#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:22
msgid "Plotting single-parameter symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:24
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` method can plot symbols via the ``style``, "
"``size``, and ``symbol`` parameters. This tutorial focuses on single-"
"parameter symbols; for an overview of the available single-parameter "
"symbols, see the Gallery example :doc:`Single-parameter symbols </gallery/"
"symbols/basic_symbols>`. The available multi-parameter symbols are explained "
"in the Gallery example :doc:`Multi-parameter symbols </gallery/symbols/"
"multi_parameter_symbols>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:33
msgid ""
"import numpy as np\n"
"import pygmt\n"
"\n"
"# Set up five sample data points as NumPy arrays for the x and y values\n"
"x = np.array([-4, -2, 0, 2, 4])\n"
"y = np.array([0, 0, 0, 0, 0])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:47
msgid "Plot single-parameter symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:49
msgid ""
"Use the ``style`` parameter of the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot` method to plot "
"all data points with the same symbol and size. By default, the symbol is "
"drawn unfilled with an 0.25-points, thick, solid outline. Use the ``pen`` "
"parameter to adjust the outline by providing a string argument in the form "
"*width*,\\ *color*,\\ *style*; for details see the Gallery example :doc:"
"`Line styles </gallery/lines/linestyles>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:57
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Plot circles (first \"c\") with a diameter of 0.5 centimeters (second "
"\"c\")\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, style=\"c0.5c\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Adjust the outline via the pen parameter\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, style=\"c0.5c\", pen=\"1p,orange\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:77
msgid ""
"Use the ``fill`` the parameter to add a fill color or pattern. Note, that no "
"outline is drawn by default when ``fill`` is used. For the available "
"patterns and their usage see :class:`pygmt.params.Pattern` and the Gallery "
"example :doc:`Bit and hachure patterns </gallery/symbols/patterns>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:84
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Add a color via the fill parameter\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, style=\"c0.5c\", fill=\"gray\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.shift_origin(xshift=\"w+1c\")\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"# Add an outline via the pen parameter\n"
"fig.plot(x=x, y=y, style=\"c0.5c\", fill=\"gray\", pen=\"1p,orange\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:106
msgid "Use individual sizes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:108
msgid ""
"Use the ``size`` parameter to plot the data points with individual sizes. "
"Provide the different sizes as a NumPy array or array-like object of "
"integers or floats."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:113
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(\n"
"    x=x,\n"
"    y=y,\n"
"    # Plot circles (first \"c\") with a diameter in centimeters (second "
"\"c\")\n"
"    style=\"cc\",\n"
"    size=[0.5, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.3],  # Use individual sizes\n"
"    fill=\"gray\",\n"
"    pen=\"1p,orange\",\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:136
msgid "Use individual symbols"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:138
msgid ""
"Use the ``symbol`` parameter to plot the data points with individual "
"symbols. Provide the different symbols as a list of strings. Here, only "
"single-parameter symbols can be used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:144
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(\n"
"    x=x,\n"
"    y=y,\n"
"    # Use a constant size of 0.5 centimeters\n"
"    style=\"0.5c\",\n"
"    # Plot a circle, a square, a triangle, an inverse triangle, a diamond\n"
"    symbol=[\"c\", \"s\", \"t\", \"i\", \"d\"],\n"
"    fill=\"gray\",\n"
"    pen=\"1p,orange\",\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:168
msgid "Use individual symbols and sizes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:170
msgid ""
"Use the ``symbol`` and ``size`` parameters together to plot the data points "
"with individual symbols and sizes. The arguments passed to ``symbol`` and "
"``size`` must have the same length. The unit for ``size`` is now set via the "
"GMT default parameter ``PROJ_LENGTH_UNIT`` which can by adjusted using :"
"class:`pygmt.config` [Default is centimeters]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/basics/symbols.rst:178
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.basemap(region=[-5, 5, -2, 2], projection=\"X10c/4c\", frame=True)\n"
"\n"
"fig.plot(\n"
"    x=x,\n"
"    y=y,\n"
"    symbol=[\"c\", \"s\", \"t\", \"i\", \"d\"],\n"
"    size=[0.5, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.3],\n"
"    fill=\"gray\",\n"
"    pen=\"1p,orange\",\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

